+          "spanish": "Se suele llamar populismo a los movimientos que hablaban en nombre del pueblo.",
+          "english": "The movements that spoke in the name of the people are usually called populism."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Se suele señalar, entre las características típicas de estos movimientos, la centralidad de un líder carismático.",
-          "english": "Among the typical characteristics of these movements, the centrality of a charismatic leader is usually pointed out."
+          "spanish": "Se suele hablar de una relación directa entre el líder y sus seguidores.",
+          "english": "People usually speak of a direct relationship between the leader and his followers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No se suele encontrar un acuerdo completo sobre si el populismo debe entenderse como un estilo, una ideología o una estrategia electoral.",
-          "english": "There usually isn't complete agreement about whether populism should be understood as a style, an ideology, or an electoral strategy."
+          "spanish": "Se dice que el líder hablaba directamente a la gente.",
+          "english": "It is said that the leader spoke directly to the people."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Lo que sí se suele reconocer, casi sin excepción, es que estos movimientos surgieron en un contexto muy particular.",
-          "english": "What is usually acknowledged, almost without exception, is that these movements arose in a very particular context."
+          "spanish": "No se suele llegar a un acuerdo sobre la definición de populismo.",
+          "english": "People do not usually reach an agreement on the definition of populism."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Se suele* + infinitive is built from soler (to usually do something) plus the impersonal se -- don't confuse it with the simpler suele + infinitive, which needs a stated subject (el líder suele hablar por radio)."
+      "content": "Keep the verb singular when *se* is impersonal and the object comes with *a*: *se suele llamar populismo a los movimientos*. To say who holds the view, add *según*: *según algunos historiadores, se suele …*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-02-tanto-es-asi-que-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-02-tanto-es-asi-que-gr.json
index 5ed0df4d..16233101 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-02-tanto-es-asi-que-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-02-tanto-es-asi-que-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.populismo.02.tanto-es-asi-que",
-  "title": "Tanto es así que: reforzar una afirmación",
+  "title": "Preterite and imperfect: telling Perón's story",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Tanto es así que* (so much so that) reinforces a claim by pointing to concrete proof of just how true it was -- ideal for a reading that wants to show, not just tell, how powerful Perón's and Evita's popular support really was. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the *indefinido* and the *imperfecto* from A2 Unit 22 (the preterite vs the imperfect). Use the preterite for the events that move the story forward (*marcharon*, *habló*) and the imperfect for the background: what things were like, what was going on, a person's age. Perón's story needs both."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Perón impulsó mejoras salariales y una expansión de los derechos sindicales; tanto es así que, cuando intentaron apartarlo del poder, decenas de miles de trabajadores se movilizaron para exigir su liberación.",
-          "english": "Perón pushed for wage improvements and an expansion of union rights; so much so that, when they tried to remove him from power, tens of thousands of workers mobilized to demand his release."
+          "spanish": "Perón era coronel cuando los militares tomaron el poder en 1943.",
+          "english": "Perón was a colonel when the military took power in 1943."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Eva Duarte se convirtió en una figura de enorme influencia; tanto es así que, tras su muerte prematura, el país entero entró en un duelo verdaderamente masivo.",
-          "english": "Eva Duarte became a figure of enormous influence; so much so that, after her early death, the entire country entered into truly massive mourning."
+          "spanish": "Los obreros trabajaban mucho y tenían pocos derechos.",
+          "english": "The workers worked a lot and had few rights."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El gobierno combinó redistribución con métodos autoritarios; tanto es así que los historiadores siguen debatiendo cómo entender el peronismo.",
-          "english": "The government combined redistribution with authoritarian methods; so much so that historians still debate how to understand Peronism."
+          "spanish": "Mientras la multitud esperaba en la plaza, Perón habló desde un balcón.",
+          "english": "While the crowd was waiting in the square, Perón spoke from a balcony."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Aquel episodio se convirtió en la fecha fundacional del movimiento peronista, y Perón ganó las elecciones apenas unos meses después.",
-          "english": "That episode became the founding date of the Peronist movement, and Perón won the elections just a few months later."
+          "spanish": "Eva Perón murió en 1952, cuando tenía 33 años.",
+          "english": "Eva Perón died in 1952, when she was 33."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Tanto es así que* always follows the claim it's reinforcing, usually after a semicolon or period -- it never opens a paragraph on its own, since it needs something concrete to point back to."
+      "content": "Ask yourself: is this a step in the story (preterite) or the scene around it (imperfect)? A date or a single moment (*en 1952*, *el 17 de octubre*) usually goes with the preterite; *mientras* + a long background action goes with the imperfect."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-03-a-diferencia-de-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-03-a-diferencia-de-gr.json
index 464067c2..ce55657f 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-03-a-diferencia-de-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-03-a-diferencia-de-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.populismo.03.a-diferencia-de",
-  "title": "A diferencia de: comparar dos casos",
+  "title": "Cuyo: whose government, whose laws",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*A diferencia de* (unlike, in contrast to) opens a direct comparison between two cases -- exactly what this lesson needs to hold Cárdenas next to Perón point by point: personal style, social base, and lasting reputation. It's followed by a noun phrase, not a full clause. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *cuyo* from B1 Unit 5: it links a person or thing to something that belongs to it, and it means \"whose\". It agrees with the noun that comes after it (*cuyo gobierno*, *cuya constitución*, *cuyos jefes*, *cuyas leyes*), not with the owner. It is a good tool for comparing Cárdenas in Mexico with Vargas in Brazil."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "A diferencia del carismático coronel argentino, Cárdenas era conocido por un estilo personal más bien discreto, casi austero.",
-          "english": "Unlike the charismatic Argentine colonel, Cárdenas was known for a rather understated, almost austere personal style."
+          "spanish": "Cárdenas fue un presidente cuyo gobierno repartió tierras a los campesinos.",
+          "english": "Cárdenas was a president whose government gave land to the peasants."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A diferencia de Perón, que construyó su poder principalmente sobre el movimiento obrero urbano, Cárdenas se apoyó de manera decisiva en el campesinado.",
-          "english": "Unlike Perón, who built his power mainly on the urban labor movement, Cárdenas relied decisively on the peasantry."
+          "spanish": "México era un país cuya constitución no permitía la reelección.",
+          "english": "Mexico was a country whose constitution did not allow re-election."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A diferencia de lo que muchos analistas internacionales predijeron, esta medida no provocó el colapso económico que se temía.",
-          "english": "Unlike what many international analysts predicted, this measure didn't cause the economic collapse that was feared."
+          "spanish": "Vargas fue un presidente cuyas leyes protegieron a los trabajadores.",
+          "english": "Vargas was a president whose laws protected the workers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "A diferencia también del peronismo, el legado de Cárdenas resulta mucho más ampliamente reconocido y celebrado dentro de México.",
-          "english": "Also unlike Peronism, Cárdenas's legacy turns out to be much more widely recognized and celebrated within Mexico."
+          "spanish": "Los campesinos cuyas familias recibieron tierras formaron ejidos.",
+          "english": "The peasants whose families received land formed ejidos."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*A diferencia de* lo que + verb (as in the third example) is a handy variant for contrasting with an expectation or prediction rather than another person or thing -- lo que stands in for \"what [people expected/predicted].\""
+      "content": "Never put an article between *cuyo* and its noun: *cuyo gobierno*, not *cuyo el gobierno*. Do not replace it with *que su*: *un presidente que su gobierno…* is a common mistake."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-04-es-cuestionable-que-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-04-es-cuestionable-que-gr.json
index 004cd8e1..0ae61959 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-04-es-cuestionable-que-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-04-es-cuestionable-que-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.populismo.04.es-cuestionable-que",
-  "title": "Subjuntivo de valoración: es cuestionable que",
+  "title": "Opinion verbs and mood: who believes what",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Es cuestionable que (it's questionable / debatable that) is an evaluative expression -- like es importante que or es lamentable que -- and always triggers the subjunctive, since it's judging a claim rather than asserting it as settled fact. It's the natural tool for a critical, analytical look at populism's mechanism. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *creo que* + indicative and *no creo que* + subjunctive from B1 Unit 7. When someone believes a claim is true, the verb after *que* is in the indicative. When they do not believe it, the verb after *que* is in the subjunctive. Historians disagree about populism, so both forms appear: *algunos historiadores creen que…; otros no creen que…*."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Es cuestionable que este \"pueblo\" invocado constantemente por los líderes populistas corresponda realmente a toda la población de un país.",
-          "english": "It's questionable whether this \"people\" constantly invoked by populist leaders really corresponds to a country's entire population."
+          "spanish": "Algunos historiadores creen que estos movimientos dieron voz a los pobres.",
+          "english": "Some historians believe that these movements gave a voice to the poor."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Muchos politólogos consideran preocupante esta relación directa entre líder y masas, ya que tiende a debilitar los mecanismos institucionales de control.",
-          "english": "Many political scientists consider this direct relationship between leader and masses concerning, since it tends to weaken institutional mechanisms of accountability."
+          "spanish": "Otros no creen que el vínculo directo sea bueno para la democracia.",
+          "english": "Others do not believe that the direct link is good for democracy."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Es también cuestionable que la radio resultara, en sí misma, un factor puramente neutral.",
-          "english": "It's also questionable whether radio itself turned out to be a purely neutral factor."
+          "spanish": "Algunos no creen que un sindicato controlado por el Estado pueda defender a los trabajadores.",
+          "english": "Some do not believe that a union controlled by the state can defend the workers."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Sería igualmente cuestionable que redujéramos el populismo latinoamericano a un simple fenómeno de manipulación de masas.",
-          "english": "It would be equally questionable to reduce Latin American populism to a simple phenomenon of mass manipulation."
+          "spanish": "Muchos creen que la participación del pueblo era real.",
+          "english": "Many believe that the participation of the people was real."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Es cuestionable que + subjunctive expresses genuine, ongoing doubt -- contrast it with es evidente que or es un hecho que, both of which take the indicative because they assert something as settled rather than debatable."
+      "content": "The subjunctive shows that the speaker does not accept the idea as a fact (*sea*, *pueda*, *tenga*). The same rule works with *no pensar que* and *no opinar que*. Add the person who holds the view: *algunos historiadores*, *otros*, *según ellos*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-05-si-bien-tambien-es-cierto-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-05-si-bien-tambien-es-cierto-gr.json
index 9d1f5060..79d0ba12 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-05-si-bien-tambien-es-cierto-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-populismo-05-si-bien-tambien-es-cierto-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.populismo.05.si-bien-tambien-es-cierto",
-  "title": "Si bien... también es cierto que: un balance equilibrado",
+  "title": "Contrast connectors: weighing the populist legacy",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Si bien* (while, although) grants one point in full before también es cierto que (it's also true that) adds a second, equally weighted point -- a formal pattern for holding two true things in balance without letting either cancel the other out, exactly what a fair verdict on populism demands. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *sin embargo* and *no obstante* from B1 Unit 1: they open a sentence that goes against the one before. You also know *aunque* from A2 Unit 28 (concessive clauses). Historians use them to weigh a legacy, because every government of this period has results that people praise and results that people criticise."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Si bien resulta innegable que estos movimientos produjeron mejoras materiales genuinas, también es cierto que debilitaron las instituciones democráticas de sus países.",
-          "english": "While it's undeniable that these movements produced genuine material improvements, it's also true that they weakened their countries' democratic institutions."
+          "spanish": "Los gobiernos ampliaron los derechos de los trabajadores. Sin embargo, concentraron mucho poder.",
+          "english": "The governments extended the rights of the workers. However, they concentrated a lot of power."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Si bien Perón y Cárdenas representan casos bastante distintos, también es cierto que ambos dejaron una huella política que sus países todavía siguen procesando.",
-          "english": "While Perón and Cárdenas represent quite different cases, it's also true that both left a political mark their countries are still processing."
+          "spanish": "La industria creció mucho. No obstante, no resolvió todos los problemas del país.",
+          "english": "Industry grew a lot. Nevertheless, it did not solve all the problems of the country."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Si bien el fenómeno suele asociarse principalmente con Argentina y México, también es cierto que surgieron movimientos comparables en Brasil, bajo Getúlio Vargas.",
-          "english": "While the phenomenon is usually associated mainly with Argentina and Mexico, it's also true that comparable movements arose in Brazil, under Getúlio Vargas."
+          "spanish": "Aunque cada país era diferente, los trabajadores tuvieron más peso en los tres casos.",
+          "english": "Although each country was different, the workers had more weight in all three cases."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Si bien resulta tentador reducir el populismo a una simple historia de héroes o villanos, también es cierto que la evidencia histórica exige un juicio más matizado.",
-          "english": "While it's tempting to reduce populism to a simple story of heroes or villains, it's also true that the historical evidence demands a more nuanced judgment."
+          "spanish": "Los seguidores apoyaban al líder; sin embargo, los críticos hablaban de un peligro.",
+          "english": "The followers supported the leader; however, the critics spoke of a danger."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Both halves of *si bien*... también es cierto que take the indicative here, since each clause states something the writer accepts as fact -- unlike aunque + subjunctive, this pattern isn't conceding a hypothetical, it's affirming two real, competing truths."
+      "content": "*Sin embargo* and *no obstante* start a new sentence (or follow a semicolon) and are followed by a comma. *Aunque* joins two ideas in one sentence and, for a fact, takes the indicative."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-01-lejos-de-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-01-lejos-de-gr.json
index 75842b12..3547027f 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-01-lejos-de-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-01-lejos-de-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.revolucioncubana.01.lejos-de",
-  "title": "Lejos de + infinitivo: corregir una idea equivocada",
+  "title": "Soler: how things used to be in Cuba",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Lejos de* + infinitive (far from doing X) opens a sentence by rejecting a natural but wrong assumption before giving the real picture -- exactly what pre-revolutionary Cuba needs, since \"poor, backward island\" isn't the accurate starting point. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *soler* + infinitive from B1 Unit 5: it says that someone usually does something, and in the imperfect it means \"used to\". It is the natural tool to describe the habits of a whole society before a big change, as in Cuba before 1959."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Lejos de ser uno de los países más pobres de la región, Cuba contaba con uno de los ingresos per cápita más altos de América Latina.",
-          "english": "Far from being one of the region's poorest countries, Cuba had one of the highest per-capita incomes in Latin America."
+          "spanish": "Los trabajadores del azúcar solían trabajar solo durante la zafra.",
+          "english": "The sugar workers used to work only during the harvest."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Lejos de reflejar una prosperidad ampliamente compartida, esta riqueza convivía con una desigualdad social profunda.",
-          "english": "Far from reflecting widely shared prosperity, this wealth coexisted with deep social inequality."
+          "spanish": "Los estudiantes solían reunirse y protestar contra el gobierno.",
+          "english": "The students used to meet and protest against the government."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Lejos de ser una economía plenamente independiente, la de Cuba dependía casi por completo de una cuota especial de exportación de azúcar hacia Estados Unidos.",
-          "english": "Far from being a fully independent economy, Cuba's depended almost entirely on a special sugar export quota to the United States."
+          "spanish": "Antes de 1959 los gobiernos solían tener problemas de corrupción.",
+          "english": "Before 1959 governments used to have problems of corruption."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Esta combinación reunía, precisamente, las condiciones que analizamos como precondiciones típicas para una revolución genuina.",
-          "english": "This combination brought together precisely the conditions we analyzed as typical preconditions for a genuine revolution."
+          "spanish": "Muchos turistas solían visitar La Habana.",
+          "english": "Many tourists used to visit Havana."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Lejos de* always sets up a contrast: state the rejected idea first, then follow with the accurate one, usually introduced by *sin embargo* or a comma-separated clause, as every example above does."
+      "content": "*Soler* is used almost only in the present (*suele*) and the imperfect (*solía*). Do not add a preposition: *solían trabajar*, not *solían a trabajar*. For one event that happened once, use the preterite instead: *Batista tomó el poder en 1952*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-02-acabar-por-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-02-acabar-por-gr.json
index 02cfd346..da10d296 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-02-acabar-por-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-02-acabar-por-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.revolucioncubana.02.acabar-por",
-  "title": "Acabar por + infinitivo: cómo terminó un proceso",
+  "title": "Pluperfect: what had already happened",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*Acabar por* + infinitive (to end up doing something) traces a winding process to its eventual outcome -- perfect for a story that goes from a failed barracks attack to a shipwrecked landing to two years of guerrilla war before finally reaching Havana. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the *pluscuamperfecto* from B1 Unit 1: *había* + past participle says that something had already happened before another moment in the past. In the story of the revolution it shows what the rebels had already done when something else happened: *cuando Batista huyó, los rebeldes ya habían ganado importantes batallas*."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El ataque fracasó casi por completo, y Castro acabó por pasar casi dos años en prisión.",
-          "english": "The attack failed almost completely, and Castro ended up spending nearly two years in prison."
+          "spanish": "Cuando Batista huyó, los rebeldes ya habían ganado importantes batallas.",
+          "english": "When Batista fled, the rebels had already won important battles."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Castro organizó un pequeño grupo armado que acabó por desembarcar en Cuba en diciembre de 1956.",
-          "english": "Castro organized a small armed group that ended up landing in Cuba in December 1956."
+          "spanish": "Los rebeldes que habían sobrevivido se refugiaron en la Sierra Maestra.",
+          "english": "The rebels who had survived took refuge in the Sierra Maestra."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Los pocos sobrevivientes acabaron por refugiarse en las montañas de la Sierra Maestra, iniciando una guerra de guerrillas prolongada.",
-          "english": "The few survivors ended up taking refuge in the Sierra Maestra mountains, starting a prolonged guerrilla war."
+          "spanish": "Che Guevara era un médico argentino que había llegado a Cuba en el Granma.",
+          "english": "Che Guevara was an Argentine doctor who had come to Cuba on the Granma."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Ante el avance imparable de las fuerzas rebeldes, Batista acabó por huir del país hacia República Dominicana.",
-          "english": "Facing the unstoppable advance of the rebel forces, Batista ended up fleeing the country to the Dominican Republic."
+          "spanish": "Castro había salido de la cárcel en 1955 y vivía en México.",
+          "english": "Castro had left prison in 1955 and was living in Mexico."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*Acabar por* + infinitive differs from *acabar de* + infinitive (which you learned earlier, meaning \"to have just done something\") -- por marks the eventual result of a process, while de marks something that just finished. Don't confuse the two."
+      "content": "Use the pluperfect for the earlier action and the preterite for the later one: *cuando huyó Batista* (later), *ya habían ganado* (earlier). Nothing goes between *había* and the participle: *ya habían ganado*, not *habían ya ganado*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-03-cada-vez-menos-mas-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-03-cada-vez-menos-mas-gr.json
index 09097201..7bf6099f 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-03-cada-vez-menos-mas-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-03-cada-vez-menos-mas-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.revolucioncubana.03.cada-vez-menos-mas",
-  "title": "Cada vez menos... cada vez más: describir una radicalización",
+  "title": "Cuanto más… más…: changes that grow together",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Cada vez menos... cada vez más (less and less... more and more) narrates two trends moving in opposite directions at the same time -- exactly the shape of Cuba's drift away from the US and toward the USSR, month by month rather than all at once. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *cuanto más…, más…* (\"the more…, the more…\") from B1 Unit 2: it links two changes that grow together. The first part is the cause or the starting point, and the second part grows with it. In Cuba's history it fits the way the relations between Cuba, the Soviet Union and the United States grew tense step by step."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El gobierno se mostraba cada vez menos dispuesto a mantener relaciones normales con las empresas estadounidenses, y cada vez más decidido a impulsar una reforma agraria radical.",
-          "english": "The government showed itself less and less willing to maintain normal relations with American companies, and more and more determined to push a radical land reform."
+          "spanish": "Cuanto más se acercaba Cuba a la Unión Soviética, más grande era la desconfianza de Estados Unidos.",
+          "english": "The closer Cuba got to the Soviet Union, the greater was the distrust of the United States."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El gobierno de Castro se volvió cada vez menos tolerante con la oposición interna, y cada vez más dispuesto a nacionalizar propiedades estadounidenses.",
-          "english": "Castro's government became less and less tolerant of internal opposition, and more and more willing to nationalize American-owned properties."
+          "spanish": "Cuanto más ayudaba el Estado a los pobres, más apoyo recibía el gobierno.",
+          "english": "The more the state helped the poor, the more support the government received."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Cuba se volvía cada vez menos dependiente del comercio estadounidense y cada vez más cercana a la Unión Soviética.",
-          "english": "Cuba became less and less dependent on American trade and closer and closer to the Soviet Union."
+          "spanish": "Cuanto más aprendían a leer los campesinos, más cartas escribían.",
+          "english": "The more the peasants learned to read, the more letters they wrote."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El gobierno se volvió cada vez menos parecido a los movimientos populistas, y cada vez más parecido a los regímenes de partido único de tipo soviético.",
-          "english": "The government became less and less like the populist movements, and more and more like Soviet-style one-party regimes."
+          "spanish": "Cuanto menos libertad había, más críticas recibía el gobierno.",
+          "english": "The less freedom there was, the more criticism the government received."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Volverse (to become) pairs especially well with this structure for a gradual personal or institutional shift -- se volvía cada vez menos X, cada vez más Y -- distinct from convertirse en, which usually marks a more definite, completed change of category."
+      "content": "*Cuanto* does not change when a verb follows it, and both parts need *más* or *menos*: *cuanto más… más…*, *cuanto menos… más…*. Use *mejor* or *peor* instead of *más bueno* or *más malo*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-04-a-punto-de-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-04-a-punto-de-gr.json
index e4f377da..27d73346 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-04-a-punto-de-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-04-a-punto-de-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.revolucioncubana.04.a-punto-de",
-  "title": "A punto de + infinitivo: al borde de que algo ocurra",
+  "title": "Estar a punto de: on the edge of the missile crisis",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Estar *a punto de* + infinitive (to be about to, on the verge of) marks the closest possible brush with something that almost happened but didn't -- exactly the language a reading about thirteen days in October 1962 needs. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know *estar a punto de* + infinitive from B1 Unit 1: it says that something was about to happen. In the *imperfecto* it often sets the scene before an event (*estaba a punto de llegar cuando…*); in the *indefinido* (*estuvo a punto de*) it says that something nearly happened. Both fit the Cuban missile crisis, when the world nearly saw a war."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El mundo entero estuvo a punto de vivir una guerra nuclear directa entre Estados Unidos y la Unión Soviética.",
-          "english": "The entire world came close to experiencing a direct nuclear war between the United States and the Soviet Union."
+          "spanish": "En octubre de 1962 el mundo estuvo a punto de entrar en una guerra nuclear.",
+          "english": "In October 1962 the world came close to entering a nuclear war."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Ambas superpotencias estuvieron a punto de entrar en un conflicto armado directo.",
-          "english": "Both superpowers came close to entering a direct armed conflict."
+          "spanish": "Algunos barcos soviéticos estaban a punto de llegar a la línea del bloqueo cuando se detuvieron.",
+          "english": "Some Soviet ships were about to reach the line of the blockade when they stopped."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Muchos historiadores consideran este el momento más peligroso de toda la Guerra Fría.",
-          "english": "Many historians consider this the most dangerous moment of the entire Cold War."
+          "spanish": "Los exiliados estaban a punto de desembarcar cuando empezó el ataque.",
+          "english": "The exiles were about to land when the attack began."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "La crisis se resolvió finalmente mediante una negociación secreta.",
-          "english": "The crisis was ultimately resolved through a secret negotiation."
+          "spanish": "El bloqueo estuvo a punto de causar un choque entre los barcos.",
+          "english": "The blockade almost caused a clash between the ships."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Estuvo *a punto de* + infinitive always implies the event did NOT actually happen -- estuvo *a punto de* vivir una guerra nuclear means the war was narrowly avoided, not that it broke out. Don't confuse it with *acabar por*, which marks something that did eventually happen."
+      "content": "*Estar a punto de* is always followed by an infinitive (*estaba a punto de llegar*). The action did not happen, or had not happened yet: use *estaba a punto de* to set the scene and a preterite for the event that came next."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-05-no-por-deja-de-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-05-no-por-deja-de-gr.json
index 5daebcbf..473388a3 100644
--- a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-05-no-por-deja-de-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-revolucioncubana-05-no-por-deja-de-gr.json
@@ -1,35 +1,35 @@
 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.revolucioncubana.05.no-por-deja-de",
-  "title": "No por... deja de: matizar sin negar del todo",
+  "title": "The ser passive: what was done to Cuba",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "No por + [reason] + deja de + infinitive (\"just because X, it doesn't stop being Y\") concedes a limiting fact without letting it cancel out a larger truth -- the perfect closing move for weighing Cuba's small size and its guerrillas' frequent failures against its outsized regional impact. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "content": "You know the passive with *ser* + past participle from B1 Unit 18: *La ley fue aprobada por el Parlamento*. The participle agrees with the subject (*Cuba fue suspendida*, *los misiles fueron retirados*), and *por* names who did it. It is useful for the relations between Cuba and Latin America, where the important thing is what was done to Cuba."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "No por haber triunfado en un país relativamente pequeño deja de haber sido la Revolución cubana un fenómeno de alcance verdaderamente continental.",
-          "english": "Just because it triumphed in a relatively small country doesn't mean the Cuban Revolution wasn't a phenomenon of truly continental reach."
+          "spanish": "En 1962 Cuba fue suspendida de la OEA.",
+          "english": "In 1962 Cuba was suspended from the OAS."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No por haber fracasado la mayoría de estos intentos guerrilleros deja de haber transformado profundamente el mapa político de la región.",
-          "english": "Just because most of these guerrilla attempts failed doesn't mean they didn't profoundly transform the region's political map."
+          "spanish": "La Alianza para el Progreso fue creada por Estados Unidos en 1961.",
+          "english": "The Alliance for Progress was created by the United States in 1961."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Cuba demostró que un cambio revolucionario genuino era, después de todo, posible.",
-          "english": "Cuba showed that genuine revolutionary change was, after all, possible."
+          "spanish": "Esa decisión fue tomada por la mayoría de los países miembros.",
+          "english": "That decision was taken by most of the member countries."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Prácticamente ningún gobierno latinoamericano pudo ignorar la sombra de la revolución que había triunfado en la isla en 1959.",
-          "english": "Practically no Latin American government could ignore the shadow of the revolution that had triumphed on the island in 1959."
+          "spanish": "El Che Guevara fue capturado por el ejército boliviano en 1967.",
+          "english": "Che Guevara was captured by the Bolivian army in 1967."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "The subject of both halves in no por... deja de must be the same -- here, \"the Cuban Revolution\" (haber triunfado... haber sido) -- since it's one thing whose small scale doesn't cancel out its large impact, not two different things being compared."
+      "content": "Make the participle agree with the subject: *Cuba fue suspendida*, *los guerrilleros fueron capturados*. Add *por* + the person or group only when it matters: *fue capturado por el ejército*."
     }
   ]
 }
